To solve the congruence \(30x \equiv 42 \pmod{47}\), we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 30 modulo 47. The multiplicative inverse of 30 modulo 47 is an integer \(y\) such that \(30y \equiv 1 \pmod{47}\). We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse.

First, we apply the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 30 and 47:
\[
47 = 1 \cdot 30 + 17
\]
\[
30 = 1 \cdot 17 + 13
\]
\[
17 = 1 \cdot 13 + 4
\]
\[
13 = 3 \cdot 4 + 1
\]
\[
4 = 4 \cdot 1 + 0
\]
The gcd is 1, and we can work backwards to express 1 as a combination of 30 and 47:
\[
1 = 13 - 3 \cdot 4
\]
Substitute \(4 = 17 - 13\) into the equation:
\[
1 = 13 - 3 \cdot (17 - 13) = 4 \cdot 13 - 3 \cdot 17
\]
Substitute \(13 = 30 - 17\) into the equation:
\[
1 = 4 \cdot (30 - 17) - 3 \cdot 17 = 4 \cdot 30 - 7 \cdot 17
\]
Substitute \(17 = 47 - 30\) into the equation:
\[
1 = 4 \cdot 30 - 7 \cdot (47 - 30) = 11 \cdot 30 - 7 \cdot 47
\]
Thus, we have \(11 \cdot 30 \equiv 1 \pmod{47}\), so the multiplicative inverse of 30 modulo 47 is 11. Now we can solve the original congruence by multiplying both sides by 11:
\[
30x \equiv 42 \pmod{47}
\]
\[
11 \cdot 30x \equiv 11 \cdot 42 \pmod{47}
\]
\[
x \equiv 462 \pmod{47}
\]
We reduce 462 modulo 47:
\[
462 \div 47 = 9 \text{ remainder } 39
\]
So, \(462 \equiv 39 \pmod{47}\). Therefore, the smallest positive integer that satisfies the congruence is:
\[
\boxed{39}
\]

